WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.032 --> 00:00:09.943
Today, it is common to see people on the street, riding the subway, or in the gym listening to music on their MP3 players through headphones.

2
00:00:09.944 --> 00:00:15.567
But most of us don't realize that personal stereo systems are a modern invention.

3
00:00:15.568 --> 00:00:22.456
In fact, the recording industry, which records music and sells it to the public, is not very old at all.

4
00:00:22.457 --> 00:00:24.003
Live Music

5
00:00:24.004 --> 00:00:29.434
For thousands of years, music has been an important part of every culture on the planet.

6
00:00:29.435 --> 00:00:35.616
Before the Middle Ages, however, the only kind of music that was available had to be played live.

7
00:00:35.617 --> 00:00:45.535
Music was not recorded or written down, so the knowledge of how to play instruments and certain songs was passed down orally from teacher to student.

8
00:00:45.536 --> 00:00:55.240
Then, sometime before the fifteenth century, people began to use a written language to record the notes of songs so that they could remember them and share them with others.

9
00:00:55.241 --> 00:01:00.073
This language is called musical notation, and it is still used today.

10
00:01:00.074 --> 00:01:09.955
The advent of musical notation meant that musicians in France, for example, could learn how to play a piece of music written by musicians in England without ever having met them.

11
00:01:09.956 --> 00:01:11.328
The Phonograph

12
00:01:11.329 --> 00:01:18.587
The next major advance in music technology occurred in the late nineteenth century, when Thomas Edison invented the phonograph.

13
00:01:18.588 --> 00:01:22.461
This device allowed people to record sounds for the first time.

14
00:01:22.462 --> 00:01:32.423
It had two major components: a large metal horn, which focused sound waves onto a vibrating needle, and a rotating cylinder covered in wax.

15
00:01:32.424 --> 00:01:36.484
As the needle vibrated, it would make grooves in the wax.

16
00:01:36.485 --> 00:01:40.969
The process could be reversed to replay the sound for an audience.

17
00:01:40.970 --> 00:01:48.689
People could now play their favorite songs whenever they wanted, so they didn't have to rely on live musicians anymore.

18
00:01:48.690 --> 00:01:55.441
In the beginning of the twentieth century, the cylinder was replaced with a spinning disk called a "78."

19
00:01:55.442 --> 00:02:03.530
This phonograph changed the type of music that people listened to because a 78 could record only three minutes on each side.

20
00:02:03.531 --> 00:02:07.125
So, many songs were shortened to fit the format of the medium.

21
00:02:07.126 --> 00:02:10.864
Also, the quality of the phonograph recording was quite low.

22
00:02:10.865 --> 00:02:15.460
Musicians had to play loudly to ensure that their music would be picked up.

23
00:02:15.461 --> 00:02:21.224
Higher tones, such as the voices of sopranos, were easier to record than low tones.

24
00:02:21.225 --> 00:02:25.597
Among the orchestral instruments, trumpets and violins worked best.

25
00:02:25.598 --> 00:02:29.122
For folk music, banjos were better than guitars.

26
00:02:29.123 --> 00:02:36.986
As a result, the first music to be recorded, such as the tunes played by jazz bands, featured these kinds of instruments.

27
00:02:36.987 --> 00:02:38.336
Radio

28
00:02:38.337 --> 00:02:43.559
By 1926, a new invention, the radio, was spreading across the country.

29
00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:50.821
The old mechanical equipment was replaced by microphones, which are more sensitive and can pick up a wider range of tones.

30
00:02:50.822 --> 00:02:58.376
Because singers and musicians no longer had to play at top volume, more delicate and complex styles could be captured.

31
00:02:58.377 --> 00:03:07.186
Bing Crosby, who eventually made more than 1,700 recordings, was among the first singers to take advantage of these developments.

32
00:03:07.187 --> 00:03:14.798
Instead of singing as if he were performing in a large concert hall, he sang softly into the microphone in an intimate style.

33
00:03:14.799 --> 00:03:21.388
He and other male singers like him, including Frank Sinatra, dominated the airwaves for years.

34
00:03:21.389 --> 00:03:27.464
Their popularity soared even higher when smaller, portable radios became widely available.

35
00:03:27.465 --> 00:03:32.747
Disc jockeys, often called DJs, would ask their listeners about their favorite tunes.

36
00:03:32.748 --> 00:03:42.787
Over time, this process resulted in "top-ten" lists of the most requested songs of the day, which would become known as pop─short for "popular"─music.

37
00:03:42.788 --> 00:03:51.676
Around the same time, a recording company called RCA Victor introduced albums that were more durable and able to hold more information.

38
00:03:51.677 --> 00:03:55.952
These new long-playing records, called LPs, also sounded better.

39
00:03:55.953 --> 00:04:00.990
The recordings could be more subtle and complex, and up to 23 minutes long.

40
00:04:00.991 --> 00:04:08.177
These were sold by the millions and helped launch the careers of hundreds of folk, country, blues, and pop singers.

41
00:04:08.178 --> 00:04:10.248
Les Paul and Tape

42
00:04:10.249 --> 00:04:21.506
Another development in the recording industry came after the Second World War when American engineer Les Paul was introduced to a piece of equipment used by the German military.

43
00:04:21.507 --> 00:04:29.001
It used thin strips of paper or plastic coated with metal particles arranged in a way that represents sounds.

44
00:04:29.002 --> 00:04:35.708
Significantly, these tapes could be used to record sounds, erased, and then used to record again.

45
00:04:35.709 --> 00:04:40.635
This meant that musicians didn't have to make their performances perfect the first time.

46
00:04:40.636 --> 00:04:44.080
If they made a mistake, they could just try again.

47
00:04:44.081 --> 00:04:51.402
Tape had another benefit as well; you could record several sounds separately, and combine them into the final recording later.

48
00:04:51.403 --> 00:04:57.339
This clever technique, multi-tracking, dominated professional recording for decades.

49
00:04:57.340 --> 00:05:06.440
After he mastered the art of recording, Paul went on to invent another musical tool: he built the first solid-body electric guitar.

50
00:05:06.441 --> 00:05:11.051
This new instrument was loud and unlike anything else ever heard!

51
00:05:11.052 --> 00:05:20.225
It appealed to young people, especially during the 1950s and '60s, and contributed to a new genre of music called rock and roll.

52
00:05:20.226 --> 00:05:26.605
This genre was transformed─as was the music industry yet again─in the 1970s and '80s.

53
00:05:26.606 --> 00:05:30.478
After the mid-1960s, the cassette tape was developed.

54
00:05:30.479 --> 00:05:36.139
Much smaller than regular recording tape, it was placed in a plastic shell for protection.

55
00:05:36.140 --> 00:05:45.586
Because it was so compact in size and simple to use, cassette tape gave people the chance to record their music easily and share it with friends.

56
00:05:45.587 --> 00:05:50.590
This led to a surge in the number and variety of artists on the music scene.

57
00:05:50.591 --> 00:05:53.438
The Synthesizer and Digital Technology

58
00:05:53.439 --> 00:05:57.596
Most people were satisfied with both LPs and cassette tapes.

59
00:05:57.597 --> 00:06:07.470
In 1960, however, an enterprising engineer, Robert Moog, built a machine that could make sounds out of electrical signals, called a synthesizer.

60
00:06:07.471 --> 00:06:14.397
At first, it was slow to catch on, but in the 1980s, people began to buy these machines for their homes.

61
00:06:14.398 --> 00:06:20.492
Because the synthesizer was easily hooked up to a computer and simple to play, it took off.

62
00:06:20.493 --> 00:06:29.159
This new digital technology allowed people to manipulate rhythms and beats, expanding several musical genres such as techno and hip hop.

63
00:06:29.160 --> 00:06:36.436
Meanwhile, recording companies were desperate to find a medium that could last longer and capture higher-quality recordings.

64
00:06:36.437 --> 00:06:43.833
Finally, in 1982, Sony and Phillips released a new kind of record, the compact disc (CD).

65
00:06:43.834 --> 00:06:50.914
Shaped like a smaller LP, the CD could hold over an hour of music, and it sounded crystal clear.

66
00:06:50.915 --> 00:06:59.546
The CD remained the most popular music medium for over two decades, but recently, the Internet has begun to have a profound effect on the industry.

67
00:06:59.547 --> 00:07:06.577
Since 1991, computer users have been able to compress sound into small files called MP3s.

68
00:07:06.578 --> 00:07:13.790
These can be stored on a portable storage device, such as an iPod, or sent to other users over the Internet.

69
00:07:13.791 --> 00:07:19.805
Because the files are digital data, they can be erased, modified, and copied easily.

70
00:07:19.806 --> 00:07:31.913
More importantly, MP3s can be uploaded and downloaded from the Internet, so music lovers can shop for a wide variety of songs from anywhere in the world as long as they have an Internet connection.

71
00:07:31.914 --> 00:07:38.525
With this level of convenience, it's no wonder that MP3s are quickly replacing the CD in popularity.

72
00:07:38.526 --> 00:07:41.937
The recording industry will undoubtedly continue to change.

73
00:07:41.938 --> 00:07:48.774
The standards of consumers keep on rising and the quality of recordings must continue to meet those expectations.

74
00:07:48.775 --> 00:07:53.039
Who knows what heights recorded sound will reach in the next century?

